    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          This guide is deliberately structured around questions rather than answers. Treadmill
          brand pages that assert specifications they have not verified are worse than useless,
          because they read as authoritative — so what follows is the framework we apply to any
          Horizon machine, and the questions it tells you to put to a retailer before you buy.
        </p>

        <div id="reading-a-lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Read a Treadmill Lineup</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill model numbers are not designed to be informative to shoppers, and Horizon is
            no exception. Before comparing any two machines in a lineup, establish three things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the number encodes.</strong> Most manufacturers use a series prefix and a
            tier number, where the prefix indicates the product family and the number indicates
            position within it. What that does not tell you is whether two machines share a frame,
            a motor or a deck — which is the thing that actually determines how they compare.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where the model-year boundary falls.</strong> Annual refreshes in this category
            are overwhelmingly console and software changes, with frame, motor, deck and rollers
            carried over unchanged. That makes the outgoing model year frequently the best value in
            any lineup, and it makes "the latest model" a weak reason to pay more. Ask the retailer
            explicitly what changed between years, and treat a vague answer as evidence that the
            answer is the screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which retailer-specific variants exist.</strong> The same chassis is often sold
            under different model numbers at different retailers, occasionally with a genuine
            specification difference and more often without. This is the single most common source
            of confusion when comparing prices across sellers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-verify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Seven Things to Verify Before Buying</h2>
<p>
            These are the specifications that determine whether a machine suits you, listed in the
            order we would establish them. Every one of them should come from the manufacturer or
            the retailer in writing, for the exact model and model year you are buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            One machine in the lineup has already been through this process and has a published
            review: the <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101</a>,
            whose figures were cross-checked against the manufacturer listing, retailer listings and
            independent coverage. Use it as the worked example of what the seven questions below
            look like when they have been answered.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One: deck length and width.</strong> Sixty inches is the running threshold for
            anyone over about 5'8"; 55 is walking and light jogging. Ask for the usable flat deck
            measurement rather than the belt figure, which runs one to two inches longer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two: continuous-duty horsepower.</strong> Not peak. If only a peak figure is
            quoted, the continuous rating is unflattering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three: roller diameter.</strong> The least advertised and most predictive
            longevity specification. Budget machines use 1.9 inches, good ones 2.5, commercial
            3 or more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Four: assembled weight.</strong> The best available proxy for frame rigidity,
            which is the most immediately perceptible difference between machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Five: weight capacity</strong>, treated as 50 to 75 lbs lower than stated,
            because it is a warranty boundary rather than a comfortable operating point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Six: subscription position</strong> — specifically what the console does with
            nothing paid, and whether automatic incline control sits behind the paywall.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Seven: warranty terms</strong>, especially the labour period and whether
            service is in-home or return-to-base. Returning a 250 lb machine is not a real remedy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscription-question" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Subscription Question</h2>
<p>
            This is the question we would settle first on any modern treadmill, because it changes
            the cost of ownership more than any hardware difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            A subscription at around $39 a month is roughly $2,350 over five years — for most
            machines in the mid-market, more than the treadmill itself cost. Two machines that look
            $500 apart on a comparison page can be $2,850 apart in reality.
          </p>
          <p>
            There are three positions a manufacturer can take, and it is worth establishing which
            applies to the specific model rather than to the brand, because it varies within
            lineups. Some machines have no subscription at all. Some offer optional content while
            retaining full manual speed and incline control unpaid. And some are
            subscription-dependent, where the console does very little without a fee and you have
            paid a premium for a screen you can no longer use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to subscription-free treadmills</a>
            covers the five questions to ask a retailer, and what each answer means for the
            five-year figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-structure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Reading a Warranty Properly</h2>
<p>
            Warranty terms are the most honest specification on any listing, because they are the
            manufacturer stating in writing how long they expect their own components to last. They
            are also routinely misread.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame warranties</strong> of ten years or lifetime look generous and cover the
            component least likely to fail. A treadmill frame very rarely breaks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts warranties</strong> cover what does fail — motors and controller boards,
            which are the expensive components and the ones that friction destroys.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Labour warranties</strong> are the ones that matter most and are usually
            shortest, often a single year. Labour is a large share of any repair, and a five-year
            parts term with one year of labour is meaningfully weaker cover than the headline
            suggests.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two clauses are worth finding: whether service is in-home or return-to-base, and
            whether documented maintenance is required. Some manufacturers can decline a motor
            claim on a deck that was clearly never lubricated, which is another reason to keep to
            the <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            and note the dates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cross-shopping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What to Cross-Shop Against</h2>
<p>
            Any mid-market treadmill brand should be compared against the alternatives at the same
            price, and the useful comparison is on the specifications above rather than on brand
            reputation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against Sole</strong>, if the appeal is buying hardware without an ecosystem
            attached. Sole's position is that machines run with no account and no fee, which makes
            them the reference point for anyone comparing five-year costs. Our
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole brand guide</a>
            covers that lineup.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against NordicTrack and ProForm</strong>, if guided content is genuinely
            wanted. Both are built around a content ecosystem, and the honest comparison there is
            subscription-inclusive. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a> and
            <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm</a>
            guides cover them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against the used market</strong>, which at this price band buys considerably
            more hardware per dollar — a machine that cost twice as much three to five years ago.
            Our <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used comparison</a>
            works through when that trade makes sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Ownership Looks Like, Whatever You Buy</h2>
<p>
            The maintenance requirements of a treadmill are essentially brand-independent, and they
            matter more to how long a machine lasts than the badge does.
          </p>
          <p>
            The dominant failure mode across every manufacturer is the same: belt-to-deck friction
            rises as the surface dries, the motor draws more current to hold speed, and sustained
            current draw destroys motors and controller boards. Lubricating every 40 to 50 hours of
            use addresses it directly, takes ten minutes, and roughly doubles a machine's life.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check your manual for deck type before applying anything — some decks are pre-waxed and
            are ruined by silicone. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-lubricant/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricant guide</a>
            covers identifying which you have, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance pillar</a>
            covers the full schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            Expect a belt and deck refresh somewhere around year seven at $250 to $500, which is
            normal wear rather than failure. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            covers the whole ownership picture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-parent-company" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Who Actually Builds a Horizon</h2>
<p>
            Horizon is not an independent manufacturer, and knowing who stands behind it is more
            useful than any single specification — because it is what decides whether the machine
            can be kept running in year eight.
          </p>
          <p>
            The brand belongs to <strong>Johnson Health Tech</strong>, a large Taiwanese
            manufacturer that has been building fitness equipment at scale for decades. It also
            owns <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix Fitness</a>,
            its commercial line, and Vision Fitness. It built the
            <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LiveStrong Fitness</a>
            range under licence until that partnership ended. And in 2024 it acquired the
            <a href="/bowflex-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">BowFlex</a> assets out
            of that company's Chapter 11.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>So one parts operation now sits behind an unusual number of badges.</strong>
            For an owner that is straightforwardly good news. Components are frequently shared or
            closely related across these lines — rollers, motors, controller boards, incline motors
            and belts — which means a part unlisted under one badge may be readily available under
            another. Our <a href="/treadmill-repair/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair
            guide</a> covers how to work that cross-reference, and our LiveStrong guide is the
            worked example: a discontinued brand whose owners can still get parts precisely because
            the builder never went anywhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It also explains the house style.</strong> Horizon consoles are deliberately
            plain and subscription-free while the same company's other lines take different
            approaches, which is a positioning decision rather than a cost-cutting one. The money
            that a rival puts into a screen, Horizon tends to put into the frame, the motor and the
            warranty — and the lifetime frame and motor cover that appears across the range is the
            clearest expression of that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The practical consequence when you call for support:</strong> approach it as a
            Johnson product. Have the model and serial number from the plate on the frame, and if a
            part is not listed for your machine, ask whether the equivalent from a sibling line
            fits. That single question resolves a large share of what would otherwise look like a
            dead end.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also a reasonable argument for the brand at the point of purchase. A great deal of
            the budget and mid-market is sold by companies with no factory, no parts catalogue and
            no technician network — the class our
            <a href="/deer-run-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">marketplace brand
            guide</a> describes. Horizon is the opposite of that, and at these prices it is not a
            small distinction.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How to Use This Page Today</h2>
<p>
            Take the <strong>seven verification points</strong> to the retailer and get the answers
            in writing for the exact model and model year. Deck length and width, continuous-duty
            horsepower, roller diameter, assembled weight, weight capacity, subscription position
            and warranty terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Settle the subscription question before comparing prices</strong>, because it
            changes five-year cost more than any hardware difference in this price band.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ask what changed from last year's model</strong>, and if the answer is the
            console, buy the outgoing one.
          </p>
          <p>
            And whatever you buy,
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricate it every 40 to 50 hours</a>.
            That single habit affects lifespan more than the choice between any two machines at the
            same price.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Horizon treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Johnson Health Tech, a large Taiwanese manufacturer that also owns Matrix Fitness and Vision Fitness, built the LiveStrong Fitness range under licence, and acquired the BowFlex assets out of that company's 2024 Chapter 11. That matters practically rather than as trivia: one large parts operation sits behind several badges, and components frequently cross-reference between them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I check before buying any Horizon treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Seven things, in writing, for the exact model and model year: deck length and width, continuous-duty horsepower (not peak), roller diameter, assembled weight, weight capacity, what the console does with no subscription active, and the warranty terms — particularly the labour period and whether service is in-home.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to buy last year's treadmill model?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Frequently yes. Annual refreshes in this category are overwhelmingly console and software changes while the frame, motor, deck and rollers carry over unchanged, so the outgoing model is often mechanically identical for several hundred dollars less. Ask the retailer what changed, and treat a vague answer as meaning the screen.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a treadmill subscription add to the cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At around $39 a month it is roughly $470 a year and about $2,350 over five years — for most mid-market machines, more than the treadmill cost. Establish what the console does with nothing paid before comparing purchase prices, because two machines $500 apart can be $2,850 apart in reality.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty terms actually matter on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The parts and labour periods, not the frame. Frames very rarely fail; motors and controller boards do, and labour is a large share of any repair. A five-year parts term with one year of labour is weaker than the headline suggests. Also check whether service is in-home, since returning a 250 lb machine is not a real remedy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the brand matter for treadmill maintenance?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Barely. The dominant failure mode is identical across manufacturers: belt-to-deck friction rises, the motor draws more current, and sustained draw destroys motors and boards. Lubricating every 40 to 50 hours addresses it on any machine — check the manual first, since some decks are pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying Within Any Brand Lineup</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These apply to any manufacturer, and they are the errors that a brand page asserting unverified specifications would encourage rather than prevent.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the model number tells you what changed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Series prefixes and tier numbers indicate position within a family, not whether two machines share a frame, motor or deck. Ask specifically which components differ between two models before paying for the higher number — it is often the console and very little else.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for the current model year without asking what changed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Annual refreshes are overwhelmingly console and software updates on carried-over hardware. That makes the outgoing model frequently the best value in any lineup. Ask the retailer directly what differs, and treat an evasive answer as confirmation that the difference is the screen.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing prices across retailers without checking the model variant</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The same chassis is often sold under different model numbers at different retailers, sometimes with a genuine specification difference and often without. This is the most common reason two apparently identical machines carry different prices, and it is resolved by comparing the actual specifications rather than the numbers.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the frame warranty as the warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Ten-year and lifetime frame cover protects the component least likely to fail. What matters is the parts term, the labour term — usually the shortest — and whether service is in-home or return-to-base. Shipping a 250 lb treadmill back to a manufacturer is not a remedy anyone actually uses.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

